        "base": "https://www.wananga.com/feed/rss2",
        "language": null,
        "type": "text/html",
        "value": "<div>\n  <p>\n    <span>\n      \n           Thursday 16 April\n          \n    </span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <span>\n      <span>\n        \n            \ufeff\n           \n      </span>\n    </span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <span>\n      \n           Professor Meihana Durie, Tumuaki of Te W\u0101nanga o Raukawa, has advised that he will shortly conclude his time in the role. This reflects a number of considerations, including his ongoing academic and scholarly commitments, and his continued work in advancing M\u0101ori, iwi and hap\u016b priorities, alongside his wider work in advancing Indigenous priorities.\u00a0\n          \n    </span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <span>\n      <br />\n    </span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <span>\n      \n           Te Mana Whakahaere, the governing Council, is saddened to see Meihana depart.\u00a0 Te Amokapua, Dr Mereana Selby, said \u201cMeihana has led with professionalism and integrity. He tangata pono, he tangata k\u012b tahi, he rangatira.\u00a0 We wish Meihana and his wh\u0101nau well and sincerely thank him for his loyal service and valuable contribution.\u201d\u00a0\n          \n    </span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <span>\n      <br />\n    </span>\n  </p>\n</div>"
      }
    ],
